VB=[
X(37,'WIN OR FALL, YOU GAIN',
 S('Arjuna on one side wearing a crown, on the other rising to heaven','A','crown','EITHER WAY'),
 '“If you are killed, you will gain heaven; if you win, you will enjoy the earth. So stand up, son of Kuntī, resolved to fight.”',
 [('hataḥ vā prāpsyasi svargam','<b>killed, you gain heaven</b>'),('jitvā vā bhokṣyase mahīm','<b>winning, you enjoy the earth</b>'),('tasmāt uttiṣṭha','<b>therefore stand up</b>'),('yuddhāya kṛta-niścayaḥ','<b>resolved to fight</b>')],
 'The worldly argument ends: either outcome is good for a warrior fighting a just war.',
 'This is the warrior’s creed of the time, and Kṛṣṇa states it simply. But the very next verse lifts it: fight not for heaven or for the earth, but with an even mind. The lower argument is a stepping stone to the higher.',
 'When we have done the right thing for the right reason, every outcome has something good in it.',
 'Win or lose, <b>a just duty done is gain</b>.'),
X(38,'THE SAME IN WIN OR LOSS',
 S('A balance held perfectly level, joy and sorrow on its pans','A','scale','SAMA'),
 '“Treating alike pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat, get ready for battle. Thus you will incur no sin.”',
 [('sukha-duḥkhe same kṛtvā','<b>making joy and sorrow equal</b>'),('lābha-alābhau','<b>gain and loss</b>'),('jaya-ajayau','<b>victory and defeat</b>'),('yuddhāya yujyasva','<b>prepare for battle</b>'),('na evam pāpam avāpsyasi','<b>you will incur no sin</b>')],
 'The hinge of the chapter. Kṛṣṇa moves from “why act” to “how to act”: with an even mind.',
 'Sin, the Gītā says, lies not only in the outer deed but in the craving behind it. Act with equanimity and the act does not bind. Commentators see this verse as the bridge from Sāṅkhya (knowledge of the Self) to the yoga of action that follows.',
 'Do your best and let the outcome be. That single shift lowers stress more than almost anything else.',
 'Act with <b>an even mind</b>.'),
X(39,'NOW HEAR YOGA',
 S('Krishna turning a page from one book to another','K','book','SĀṄKHYA → YOGA'),
 '“This wisdom has been taught to you according to Sāṅkhya; now hear it according to yoga. Joined to this understanding, Pārtha, you will break the bonds of karma.”',
 [('eṣā te abhihitā','<b>this has been told to you</b>'),('sāṅkhye buddhiḥ','<b>the understanding of Sāṅkhya</b>'),('yoge tu imām śṛṇu','<b>now hear it in yoga</b>'),('buddhyā yuktaḥ','<b>joined to this understanding</b>'),('karma-bandham prahāsyasi','<b>you will cast off the bondage of karma</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa announces a new approach. Sāṅkhya here means knowledge of the Self; yoga means the method of acting rightly.',
 'Here “Sāṅkhya” does not mean the later dualist school but the path of discriminating knowledge; “yoga” is the art of action (karma yoga), not postures. The chapter’s name, Sāṅkhya Yoga, comes from this pair. The goal: freedom from the bondage that action normally creates.',
 'Knowing the truth is one half; living it in daily work is the other. The Gītā cares about both.',
 'From knowing <b>to the yoga of action</b>.'),
X(40,'NO EFFORT IS LOST',
 S('Small steps on a path, each glowing, none fading','P','seed','NO LOSS'),
 '“On this path no effort is lost and no harm arises. Even a little of this dharma protects from great fear.”',
 [('na iha abhikrama-nāśaḥ','<b>no loss of effort here</b>'),('pratyavāyaḥ na vidyate','<b>no adverse result</b>'),('svalpam api','<b>even a little</b>'),('asya dharmasya','<b>of this dharma</b>'),('trāyate mahataḥ bhayāt','<b>saves from great fear</b>')],
 'Encouragement for beginners: every honest step on this path counts.',
 'In Vedic ritual, an unfinished rite was wasted and a mistake could backfire. Kṛṣṇa says this yoga is different: nothing is wasted, no step reverses. The “great fear” is read as the fear of birth and death. Chapter 6 (6.40) returns to this promise.',
 'Start small. A little steady practice, even imperfect, is never wasted.',
 'On this path <b>no step is wasted</b>.'),
X(41,'ONE-POINTED',
 S('A single straight arrow beside a tangle of many branches','Y','bow','ONE AIM'),
 '“Here the resolute understanding is single, O joy of the Kurus; the thoughts of the irresolute are many-branched and endless.”',
 [('vyavasāya-ātmikā buddhiḥ','<b>resolute understanding</b>'),('ekā','<b>one</b>'),('kuru-nandana','<b>joy of the Kurus</b>'),('bahu-śākhāḥ','<b>many-branched</b>'),('anantāḥ ca','<b>and endless</b>'),('avyavasāyinām','<b>of the irresolute</b>')],
 'A resolved mind has one direction; an unresolved mind has endless branches.',
 'Commentators link this to the next verses: those who chase many rewards have scattered minds. One-pointedness here is not narrowness, but a mind clear about its highest aim. This becomes the basis of meditation in chapter 6.',
 'Too many goals pull us apart. Knowing what matters most simplifies everything else.',
 'A resolved mind <b>is one-pointed</b>.'),
X(42,'FLOWERY WORDS',
 S('Priests speaking in flowery bubbles about heaven','','fire','"HEAVEN! WEALTH!"'),
 '“Flowery words are spoken by the unwise, Pārtha, who delight in the letter of the Vedas and say, ‘There is nothing else.’”',
 [('yām imām puṣpitām vācam','<b>this flowery speech</b>'),('pravadanti avipaścitaḥ','<b>the unwise proclaim</b>'),('veda-vāda-ratāḥ','<b>delighting in Vedic words</b>'),('na anyat asti iti vādinaḥ','<b>saying there is nothing else</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa begins a critique of those who use the Vedas only to gain rewards. Their speech is “flowery”: attractive but without fruit.',
 'The criticism is aimed at ritualists who saw the Vedas only as means to heaven and wealth, not at the Vedas themselves; the Gītā elsewhere honours the Vedas (15.15). Commentators of all schools agree on this reading. A flower is pretty but is not yet the fruit.',
 'Even sacred things can be used selfishly. The question is always: what is it for?',
 'Pretty promises, <b>no lasting fruit</b>.'),
X(43,'DESIRE-DRIVEN RITUAL',
 S('A ladder of rituals leading up to a heaven full of pleasures','','gods','REWARDS'),
 '“Full of desires, intent on heaven, they offer birth as the fruit of action, and speak of many special rites aimed at pleasure and power.”',
 [('kāma-ātmānaḥ','<b>full of desires</b>'),('svarga-parāḥ','<b>intent on heaven</b>'),('janma-karma-phala-pradām','<b>offering birth as fruit of action</b>'),('kriyā-viśeṣa-bahulām','<b>rich in special rites</b>'),('bhoga-aiśvarya-gatim','<b>aimed at pleasure and power</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa describes such ritualists: driven by desire, aiming only at heaven, multiplying rites for enjoyment and power.',
 'Even heaven, in this view, is temporary; it leads back to birth (9.21). The aim of the Gītā is freedom, not a better reward. Rituals for heaven, however splendid, keep one turning in the wheel.',
 'Doing good for a reward is better than doing harm, but it still keeps us bargaining. The Gītā invites a freer motive.',
 'Heaven too <b>is just another reward</b>.'),
X(44,'A MIND THAT CANNOT SETTLE',
 S('A mind pulled in many directions by glittering prizes','','mind','SCATTERED'),
 '“For those attached to pleasure and power, whose minds are carried away by such words, the resolute understanding is not fixed in samādhi.”',
 [('bhoga-aiśvarya-prasaktānām','<b>of those attached to pleasure and power</b>'),('tayā apahṛta-cetasām','<b>whose minds are stolen by it</b>'),('vyavasāya-ātmikā buddhiḥ','<b>resolute understanding</b>'),('samādhau na vidhīyate','<b>is not established in samādhi</b>')],
 'Those whose minds are stolen by promises of pleasure cannot reach the steady, resolute state.',
 '<i>Samādhi</i> here means the settled state of mind, not only deep trance. Commentators say a mind divided between many desires cannot gather itself. This links back to 2.41: one-pointed against many-branched.',
 'A mind always chasing the next thing never rests. Settling our wants is part of settling our mind.',
 'Craving <b>steals the steady mind</b>.'),
X(45,'BEYOND THE THREE GUṆAS',
 S('Three coloured balls of the gunas, and a person rising above them','Y','guna','NIS-TRAIGUṆYA'),
 '“The Vedas deal with the three guṇas. Be free of the three guṇas, Arjuna: free of the pairs of opposites, ever established in purity (sattva), free of getting and keeping, possessed of the Self.”',
 [('trai-guṇya-viṣayāḥ vedāḥ','<b>the Vedas concern the three guṇas</b>'),('nis-traiguṇyaḥ bhava','<b>be beyond the three guṇas</b>'),('nir-dvandvaḥ','<b>free of opposites</b>'),('nitya-sattva-sthaḥ','<b>ever in sattva</b>'),('niryoga-kṣemaḥ','<b>free of acquiring and keeping</b>'),('ātmavān','<b>established in the Self</b>')],
 'The ritual portion of the Vedas works within the world of the three guṇas. Kṛṣṇa asks Arjuna to rise above them.',
 'The three guṇas, sattva, rajas and tamas, are explained fully in chapter 14. <i>Yoga-kṣema</i>, getting what one lacks and keeping what one has, sums up worldly anxiety. Kṛṣṇa will promise in 9.22 that he himself carries the yoga-kṣema of his devotees.',
 'So much worry is about getting and keeping. Imagine a mind free of both: that is what Kṛṣṇa offers.',
 'Rise above <b>the three guṇas</b>.'),
X(46,'A WELL IN A FLOOD',
 S('A small well next to a vast flood of water','','river','A WELL IN A FLOOD'),
 '“As much use as there is in a small well when water floods everywhere, so much is there in all the Vedas for a Brahmin who knows.”',
 [('yāvān arthaḥ','<b>as much use</b>'),('uda-pāne','<b>in a well</b>'),('sarvataḥ samplutodake','<b>when water floods everywhere</b>'),('sarveṣu vedeṣu','<b>in all the Vedas</b>'),('brāhmaṇasya vijānataḥ','<b>for a knowing Brahmin</b>')],
 'For one who knows Brahman, the ritual rewards of the Vedas are like a small well in a land flooded with water.',
 'Everything the well offers is already in the flood. Commentators read: all lesser joys are contained in the joy of knowing the Self. It is not contempt for the well; the well is just no longer needed. “Brahmin” here means one who knows Brahman.',
 'When we find deep contentment, many small cravings fall away naturally, without force.',
 'All small joys are in <b>the great joy</b>.'),
X(47,'YOUR RIGHT IS TO ACTION ALONE',
 S('A farmer sowing seeds, the harvest in a thought cloud crossed out','P','seed','DO · DON’T GRASP'),
 '“Your right is to action alone, never to its fruits. Do not let the fruit of action be your motive, nor be attached to inaction.”',
 [('karmaṇi eva adhikāraḥ te','<b>your right is only to action</b>'),('mā phaleṣu kadācana','<b>never to the fruits</b>'),('mā karma-phala-hetuḥ bhūḥ','<b>let not the fruit be your motive</b>'),('mā te saṅgaḥ astu','<b>let there be no attachment</b>'),('akarmaṇi','<b>to inaction</b>')],
 'The most famous verse of the Gītā. Four instructions in two lines: act; don’t claim the result; don’t work only for the result; and don’t make this an excuse to stop.',
 'Commentators stress the last clause: this is not permission to be passive. Śaṅkara sees it as purifying the mind for knowledge; Rāmānuja as the essence of karma yoga; many modern teachers as a key to stress-free work. Results depend on many factors beyond us; effort alone is truly ours.',
 'Plan, prepare, give your best. Then let go of the outcome. You control the effort, not the harvest.',
 'Do your work; <b>the fruit is not yours to grasp</b>.',
 ("So I shouldn't care about results?","Care enough to work well. Just don't tie your peace to the outcome.")),
X(48,'YOGA IS EVENNESS',
 S('A yogi working calmly, a level horizon glowing behind','Y','scale','SAMATVAM'),
 '“Established in yoga, do your work, Dhanañjaya, giving up attachment, the same in success and failure. Evenness of mind is called yoga.”',
 [('yoga-sthaḥ','<b>established in yoga</b>'),('kuru karmāṇi','<b>do your works</b>'),('saṅgam tyaktvā','<b>giving up attachment</b>'),('siddhi-asiddhyoḥ samaḥ','<b>same in success and failure</b>'),('samatvam yogaḥ ucyate','<b>evenness is called yoga</b>')],
 'The Gītā’s own definition of yoga: not postures, not breathing, but equanimity in action.',
 'Two definitions of yoga stand side by side: <i>samatvam</i> here and <i>karmasu kauśalam</i> in 2.50. Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra defines yoga as stilling the mind; the Gītā’s yoga is a steadiness that works in the middle of action.',
 'The same calm in success and failure: that is a goal worth a lifetime of practice.',
 '<b>Evenness of mind</b> is yoga.'),
X(49,'PITIFUL ARE THOSE WHO WORK FOR FRUIT',
 S('A person anxiously clutching a coin bag while another works calmly','P','gift','FOR REWARD ONLY?'),
 '“Action (done for fruit) is far inferior to the yoga of understanding, Dhanañjaya. Seek refuge in understanding. Pitiful are those whose motive is the fruit.”',
 [('dūreṇa hi avaram karma','<b>action is far inferior</b>'),('buddhi-yogāt','<b>to the yoga of understanding</b>'),('buddhau śaraṇam anviccha','<b>take refuge in understanding</b>'),('kṛpaṇāḥ','<b>pitiful, poor</b>'),('phala-hetavaḥ','<b>those motivated by fruit</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa contrasts mere action done for gain with action guided by understanding (<i>buddhi-yoga</i>).',
 'The word <i>kṛpaṇa</i> returns from 2.7, where Arjuna called himself pitiful. Here it describes those who work only for rewards: they are spiritually poor, however rich they become. <i>Buddhi-yoga</i>, the yoga of understanding, becomes a key term (10.10, 18.57).',
 'Working only for reward makes us anxious and small. Working from understanding makes us free.',
 'Work from understanding, <b>not for reward</b>.'),
X(50,'SKILL IN ACTION',
 S('An archer aiming calmly, arrow flying straight to the target','A','light','KAUŚALAM'),
 '“One joined to understanding leaves behind both good and evil deeds even here. So devote yourself to yoga. Yoga is skill in action.”',
 [('buddhi-yuktaḥ','<b>joined to understanding</b>'),('jahāti iha','<b>gives up here</b>'),('ubhe sukṛta-duṣkṛte','<b>both good and bad deeds</b>'),('yogāya yujyasva','<b>apply yourself to yoga</b>'),('yogaḥ karmasu kauśalam','<b>yoga is skill in action</b>')],
 'The second definition of yoga: <i>karmasu kauśalam</i>, skill in action.',
 'What skill? Commentators answer: the skill of acting so that action does not bind. Even “good deeds” bind if done with ego, by producing more karma. The phrase is now the motto of many institutions. It does not mean mere cleverness; it means acting wisely, without grasping.',
 'Do your work with full care and no clinging. That is skill in action.',
 'Yoga is <b>skill in action</b>.'),
X(51,'BEYOND THE CYCLE',
 S('A sage stepping off a turning wheel into light','Y','wheel','FREE'),
 '“The wise, joined to understanding, giving up the fruits born of action, freed from the bondage of birth, reach the state beyond all ill.”',
 [('karma-jam phalam tyaktvā','<b>giving up the fruit born of action</b>'),('manīṣiṇaḥ','<b>the wise</b>'),('janma-bandha-vinirmuktāḥ','<b>freed from the bond of birth</b>'),('padam gacchanti','<b>reach the state</b>'),('anāmayam','<b>free of all ill</b>')],
 'The result of acting without grasping: freedom from the cycle of birth, and a state free of all suffering.',
 '<i>Anāmaya</i> literally means “without disease”. Liberation is pictured as perfect health of the soul. Commentators link this to <i>mokṣa</i>, freedom, the final goal taught throughout the Gītā.',
 'Letting go of grasping is like recovering from an illness we did not know we had.',
 'Let go of the fruit, <b>and be free</b>.'),
X(52,'THROUGH THE FOG',
 S('A person walking out of a thick fog into clear sky','P','light','OUT OF THE FOG',SKY,'<ellipse cx="60" cy="90" rx="60" ry="40" fill="#bbb" opacity=".7"/>'),
 '“When your understanding crosses beyond the thicket of delusion, then you will become indifferent to what has been heard and what is yet to be heard.”',
 [('yadā te moha-kalilam','<b>when the thicket of delusion</b>'),('buddhiḥ vyatitariṣyati','<b>your understanding crosses</b>'),('tadā gantāsi nirvedam','<b>then you will reach indifference</b>'),('śrotavyasya śrutasya ca','<b>to what is to be heard and what has been heard</b>')],
 'When confusion clears, Arjuna will no longer need to weigh endless teachings and promises.',
 '“Heard and to be heard” refers to scriptural statements about rewards. Once the mind is clear, these lose their pull. Commentators note <i>nirveda</i>, a calm detachment, as a sign of maturity.',
 'When we understand something deeply, we stop needing to collect more opinions about it.',
 'Clear mind, <b>no more confusion</b>.'),
X(53,'STEADY IN SAMĀDHI',
 S('A meditator sitting perfectly still while voices swirl around','Y','mind','ACALĀ'),
 '“When your understanding, now confused by conflicting texts, stands unshaken and steady in samādhi, then you will attain yoga.”',
 [('śruti-vipratipannā','<b>confused by conflicting scripture</b>'),('te yadā sthāsyati','<b>when yours will stand</b>'),('niścalā','<b>unshaken</b>'),('samādhau acalā','<b>steady in samādhi</b>'),('tadā yogam avāpsyasi','<b>then you will attain yoga</b>')],
 'Arjuna has been confused by conflicting duties and teachings. When his mind settles, he will reach yoga.',
 'This verse prompts Arjuna’s next question. The word <i>samādhi</i> again means a settled, absorbed state. Commentators see two steps: clearing confusion (2.52) and stabilising the mind (2.53).',
 'Conflicting advice can paralyse us. A settled mind can hear all of it without being thrown.',
 'Unshaken mind: <b>that is yoga</b>.'),
X(54,'WHO IS A STHITAPRAJÑA?',
 S('Arjuna asking a question, a serene sage in a thought bubble','A','sage','HOW DO THEY LIVE?'),
 'Arjuna said: What is the mark of one of steady wisdom, Keśava, settled in samādhi? How does one of steady mind speak? How does he sit? How does he walk?',
 [('sthita-prajñasya','<b>of one of steady wisdom</b>'),('kā bhāṣā','<b>what description</b>'),('samādhi-sthasya','<b>of one settled in samādhi</b>'),('sthita-dhīḥ','<b>one of steady mind</b>'),('kim prabhāṣeta kim āsīta vrajeta kim','<b>how speak, sit, walk?</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks a practical question: what does such a person look like in daily life?',
 'The question opens the famous portrait of the <i>sthitaprajña</i>, 2.55–72. These verses were recited daily at Gandhi’s ashram prayers. Commentators note that the marks of the sage also serve as a practice for the seeker.',
 'Pick one mark of the steady-minded person and practise it today. The description is also a path.',
 'Arjuna asks: <b>what does wisdom look like?</b>'),
X(55,'CONTENT WITHIN',
 S('A sage sitting peacefully with nothing, glowing contentment','Y','light','TUṢṬAḤ'),
 'The Lord said: When one gives up all desires that arise in the mind, Pārtha, and is content in the Self by the Self alone, he is called one of steady wisdom.',
 [('prajahāti yadā kāmān','<b>when one gives up desires</b>'),('sarvān manogatān','<b>all, arising in the mind</b>'),('ātmani eva ātmanā tuṣṭaḥ','<b>content in the Self by the Self</b>'),('sthita-prajñaḥ tadā ucyate','<b>is then called of steady wisdom</b>')],
 'The first mark: freedom from craving and contentment within.',
 'Contentment “in the Self, by the Self” means a joy that needs nothing outside. Commentators distinguish this from suppression: desires fall away because a deeper joy has been found, as a child drops toys when grown.',
 'Notice moments of quiet contentment that need nothing. They are glimpses of this state.',
 'The steady-minded are <b>content within</b>.'),
X(56,'UNSHAKEN BY SORROW',
 S('A calm person while sorrow and joy rain around them','Y','storm','UNSHAKEN'),
 '“One whose mind is not disturbed in sorrow, who has no craving for pleasures, free from passion, fear and anger: he is called a sage of steady mind.”',
 [('duḥkheṣu anudvigna-manāḥ','<b>mind undisturbed in sorrows</b>'),('sukheṣu vigata-spṛhaḥ','<b>without craving in pleasures</b>'),('vīta-rāga-bhaya-krodhaḥ','<b>free of passion, fear, anger</b>'),('sthita-dhīḥ muniḥ','<b>a sage of steady mind</b>')],
 'Arjuna asked how such a person speaks; this verse describes the inner state behind their words.',
 'Three things are gone: <i>rāga</i> (passion, clinging), <i>bhaya</i> (fear) and <i>krodha</i> (anger). Commentators note these three return together in 4.10. They are the root of most troubled speech and action.',
 'Fear, clinging and anger: when one is gone, the others often weaken too.',
 'Free from <b>passion, fear and anger</b>.'),
X(57,'NEITHER DELIGHTED NOR RESENTFUL',
 S('A person receiving good news and bad news with the same calm face','Y','scale','SAME'),
 '“One who is without attachment anywhere, who neither rejoices nor resents on meeting good or bad: his wisdom is steady.”',
 [('yaḥ sarvatra anabhisnehaḥ','<b>without attachment anywhere</b>'),('tat tat prāpya śubha-aśubham','<b>meeting this or that, good or bad</b>'),('na abhinandati na dveṣṭi','<b>neither rejoices nor resents</b>'),('tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā','<b>his wisdom is established</b>')],
 'Steady wisdom shows in how one meets the good and the bad.',
 'Not rejoicing does not mean coldness. Commentators say it means no excited grasping at the good, no bitter rejection of the bad. The sage still acts to help; he is simply not thrown.',
 'Celebrate gently and grieve gently. Don’t let either carry you away.',
 'Neither <b>elated nor bitter</b>.'),
X(58,'LIKE A TORTOISE',
 S('A tortoise drawing in its head and legs','','tort','WITHDRAW'),
 '“When, like a tortoise drawing in its limbs on every side, one withdraws the senses from their objects, his wisdom is steady.”',
 [('yadā saṃharate','<b>when one withdraws</b>'),('kūrmaḥ aṅgāni iva','<b>like a tortoise its limbs</b>'),('sarvaśaḥ','<b>on all sides</b>'),('indriyāṇi indriya-arthebhyaḥ','<b>the senses from their objects</b>'),('prajñā pratiṣṭhitā','<b>wisdom is steady</b>')],
 'One of the Gītā’s most famous images: the tortoise pulls in its limbs at will.',
 'The image teaches control, not destruction. The tortoise keeps its legs and uses them; it simply withdraws them when needed. Patañjali calls this <i>pratyāhāra</i>, withdrawal of the senses, the fifth limb of yoga.',
 'Practise putting the phone down, closing the eyes, stepping back. Withdrawal at will is a learnable skill.',
 'Draw in the senses <b>at will</b>.'),
X(59,'THE TASTE REMAINS',
 S('A person fasting, a lingering taste in a thought bubble, then a bright light dissolving it','Y','food','RASA'),
 '“Sense objects fall away from one who abstains, but the taste for them remains. Even the taste falls away on seeing the Supreme.”',
 [('viṣayāḥ vinivartante','<b>objects turn away</b>'),('nirāhārasya dehinaḥ','<b>for one who abstains</b>'),('rasa-varjam','<b>except the taste</b>'),('rasaḥ api asya','<b>even his taste</b>'),('param dṛṣṭvā nivartate','<b>goes on seeing the Supreme</b>')],
 'A deep observation: giving up objects is not enough. The craving remains until something higher is seen.',
 'This verse explains why mere abstinence often fails. Fasting removes the food, not the longing. Only a higher experience, “seeing the Supreme”, dissolves the taste itself. Commentators say discipline and devotion work together.',
 'Willpower can keep us from a habit; finding something deeper makes the habit lose its taste.',
 'Only a higher joy <b>removes the taste</b>.'),
X(60,'THE TURBULENT SENSES',
 S('Wild horses pulling a chariot out of control','','chariot','TURBULENT!'),
 '“For the turbulent senses, son of Kuntī, forcibly carry away the mind even of a wise person who is striving.”',
 [('yatataḥ hi api','<b>even of one who strives</b>'),('puruṣasya vipaścitaḥ','<b>of a wise person</b>'),('indriyāṇi pramāthīni','<b>the turbulent senses</b>'),('haranti prasabham manaḥ','<b>forcibly carry away the mind</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa warns: even the wise and striving can be swept away by the senses.',
 'This is honest and humble. The Gītā does not pretend self-control is easy. The image is of wild horses (compare the Kaṭha Upaniṣad’s chariot). Commentators conclude: vigilance is needed even after progress.',
 'Don’t be surprised when you slip. Expect the senses’ pull and plan for it.',
 'Even the wise <b>can be swept away</b>.'),
X(61,'FIX THE MIND ON ME',
 S('A devotee meditating with a glowing thread to Krishna','KA','heart','MAT-PARAḤ'),
 '“Restraining them all, let him sit disciplined, intent on me. For one whose senses are under control, his wisdom is steady.”',
 [('tāni sarvāṇi saṃyamya','<b>restraining them all</b>'),('yuktaḥ āsīta','<b>let him sit disciplined</b>'),('mat-paraḥ','<b>intent on me</b>'),('vaśe hi yasya indriyāṇi','<b>whose senses are under control</b>'),('prajñā pratiṣṭhitā','<b>wisdom is steady</b>')],
 'The answer to the senses’ pull: discipline, and fixing the mind on the Lord.',
 'The word <i>mat-para</i>, “intent on me”, is the first clear hint of bhakti in the Gītā. Rāmānuja emphasises it: the senses are mastered not by force alone but by turning the heart to God. Śaṅkara reads “me” as the Self.',
 'Give the mind something good to hold, and it lets go of lesser things more easily.',
 'Fix the mind <b>on the Highest</b>.'),
X(62,'THE LADDER DOWN · I',
 S('Stairs going down: think, cling, want, anger','','mind','THINK → CLING → WANT → ANGER'),
 '“Dwelling on sense objects, a person becomes attached to them; from attachment springs desire, and from desire, anger.”',
 [('dhyāyataḥ viṣayān','<b>dwelling on objects</b>'),('puṃsaḥ','<b>of a person</b>'),('saṅgaḥ teṣu upajāyate','<b>attachment to them arises</b>'),('saṅgāt sañjāyate kāmaḥ','<b>from attachment, desire</b>'),('kāmāt krodhaḥ abhijāyate','<b>from desire, anger</b>')],
 'The first half of the famous ladder of fall: thinking leads to attachment, attachment to desire, desire to anger.',
 'It begins with mere thinking (<i>dhyāna</i>, the same word as meditation, here pointed at objects). Anger arises when desire is blocked. Commentators observe: the ladder is short and fast; the place to stop it is the first step.',
 'Notice the thought before the craving. Changing what we dwell on changes what we want.',
 'It starts <b>with a thought</b>.'),
X(63,'THE LADDER DOWN · II',
 S('Stairs continuing down: delusion, lost memory, lost reason, ruin','','mind','FOG → FORGET → RUIN'),
 '“From anger comes delusion; from delusion, loss of memory; from loss of memory, the ruin of understanding; and from the ruin of understanding, one perishes.”',
 [('krodhāt bhavati sammohaḥ','<b>from anger, delusion</b>'),('sammohāt smṛti-vibhramaḥ','<b>from delusion, confused memory</b>'),('smṛti-bhraṃśāt buddhi-nāśaḥ','<b>from lost memory, ruined reason</b>'),('buddhi-nāśāt praṇaśyati','<b>from ruined reason, one perishes</b>')],
 'The ladder continues to its end: anger clouds judgement, we forget what we know, reason fails, and we fall.',
 '“Memory” here means remembering what is right, our learning and values. In anger we forget them. Commentators call this the most practical psychology in the Gītā: it maps how a small thought can end in disaster.',
 'In a rage, pause. Anger makes us forget what we know. A breath can give memory time to return.',
 'Anger <b>makes us forget what we know</b>.'),
X(64,'FREE AMONG OBJECTS',
 S('A person walking through a market calmly, untouched by the stalls','P','food','FREE AMONG THINGS'),
 '“But one who moves among objects with senses under control, free from attraction and aversion, self-governed, attains serenity.”',
 [('rāga-dveṣa-viyuktaiḥ tu','<b>but free of attraction and aversion</b>'),('viṣayān indriyaiḥ caran','<b>moving among objects with the senses</b>'),('ātma-vaśyaiḥ','<b>with senses self-controlled</b>'),('vidheya-ātmā','<b>self-governed</b>'),('prasādam adhigacchati','<b>attains serenity</b>')],
 'The way out is not to run from the world, but to move in it freely, without likes and dislikes ruling us.',
 'Some editions read <i>vimuktaiḥ</i> for <i>viyuktaiḥ</i>; the sense is the same. <i>Prasāda</i> means clarity and grace, like water settling clear. Commentators note this verse answers Arjuna’s question “how does he walk?”: freely, among things, untouched.',
 'You don’t have to leave the world to be free. You can live fully among things without being owned by them.',
 'Free among objects, <b>serene within</b>.'),
X(65,'SERENITY ENDS SORROW',
 S('Clear still water with a smiling face reflected','Y','light','PRASĀDA'),
 '“In serenity all sorrows come to an end. For the one whose mind is serene, understanding soon becomes steady.”',
 [('prasāde','<b>in serenity</b>'),('sarva-duḥkhānām hāniḥ','<b>the end of all sorrows</b>'),('asya upajāyate','<b>arises for him</b>'),('prasanna-cetasaḥ hi āśu','<b>for the serene-minded, soon</b>'),('buddhiḥ paryavatiṣṭhate','<b>understanding is established</b>')],
 'Serenity is both the fruit and the basis: it ends sorrow and steadies understanding.',
 'A virtuous circle: clarity brings calm, calm brings more clarity. Commentators note that understanding cannot be steady in an agitated mind, as a reflection cannot be clear in rippling water.',
 'Calm first, then decide. Most of our worst decisions are made in turmoil.',
 'A serene mind <b>sees clearly</b>.'),
X(66,'NO PEACE WITHOUT DISCIPLINE',
 S('A restless person unable to sit still, no light','P','storm','NO PEACE'),
 '“For the undisciplined there is no understanding, nor meditation; without meditation there is no peace; and without peace, how can there be happiness?”',
 [('na asti buddhiḥ ayuktasya','<b>no understanding for the undisciplined</b>'),('na ca ayuktasya bhāvanā','<b>nor meditation</b>'),('na ca abhāvayataḥ śāntiḥ','<b>no peace without meditation</b>'),('aśāntasya kutaḥ sukham','<b>how happiness for the unpeaceful?</b>')],
 'A chain in the other direction: no discipline, no understanding; no meditation, no peace; no peace, no happiness.',
 '<i>Bhāvanā</i> means contemplation, holding something steadily in mind. The final question is famous: <i>aśāntasya kutaḥ sukham</i>, “how can the restless be happy?” Commentators see it as a summary of why discipline matters.',
 'Happiness needs peace; peace needs a settled mind; a settled mind needs some discipline. Start with the last.',
 'Without peace, <b>no happiness</b>.'),
X(67,'LIKE WIND ON A BOAT',
 S('A small boat on water being blown off course by wind','','storm','ADRIFT',SKY,WAVES(120)+'<path d="M80 110 h50 l-8 12 h-34z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'),
 '“When the mind follows the wandering senses, it carries away one’s understanding, as the wind carries away a boat on the water.”',
 [('indriyāṇām hi caratām','<b>of the roaming senses</b>'),('yat manaḥ anuvidhīyate','<b>when the mind follows</b>'),('tat asya harati prajñām','<b>it carries off his wisdom</b>'),('vāyuḥ nāvam iva ambhasi','<b>as wind a boat on water</b>')],
 'If the mind trails after the senses, it drags understanding off course.',
 'A clear image: the boat is understanding, the wind is the senses, and the mind is the sail. Commentators note that just one sense is enough to drive the boat astray.',
 'One distraction can steer a whole day off course. Watch which wind your mind’s sail is catching.',
 'The senses can blow us <b>off course</b>.'),
X(68,'SENSES WITHDRAWN',
 S('A tortoise and a calm sage together','Y','tort','IN CONTROL'),
 '“Therefore, mighty-armed one, the one whose senses are fully withdrawn from their objects on every side: his wisdom is steady.”',
 [('tasmāt','<b>therefore</b>'),('mahā-bāho','<b>mighty-armed one</b>'),('yasya nigṛhītāni sarvaśaḥ','<b>whose are fully restrained</b>'),('indriyāṇi indriya-arthebhyaḥ','<b>senses from their objects</b>'),('prajñā pratiṣṭhitā','<b>wisdom is steady</b>')],
 'A summary of 2.58–67: mastery of the senses is the mark of steady wisdom.',
 'Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna “mighty-armed”: you who have mastered enemies, master the senses too. Commentators note this inner victory is harder than any battle.',
 'The greatest strength is not in the arms but in self-mastery.',
 'Master the senses: <b>a greater victory</b>.'),
X(69,'NIGHT FOR SOME, DAY FOR OTHERS',
 S('A sage awake under stars while others sleep','Y','star','AWAKE',NIGHT2),
 '“What is night for all beings, in that the self-controlled is awake; and that in which beings are awake is night for the sage who sees.”',
 [('yā niśā sarva-bhūtānām','<b>what is night for all beings</b>'),('tasyām jāgarti saṃyamī','<b>in that the disciplined is awake</b>'),('yasyām jāgrati bhūtāni','<b>where beings are awake</b>'),('sā niśā paśyataḥ muneḥ','<b>that is night for the seeing sage</b>')],
 'A beautiful paradox about attention: the sage is awake to what the world sleeps through, and asleep to what the world chases.',
 'Commentators explain: the Self is “night” to the worldly, unseen; the world of objects is “night” to the sage, uninteresting. It is a reversal of values, not of hours. Many saints were mocked as fools for this reason.',
 'What we are awake to shapes our life. Are you awake to what truly matters?',
 'The sage is awake <b>where the world sleeps</b>.'),
X(70,'LIKE THE OCEAN',
 S('Rivers pouring into a calm ocean that stays level','','ocean','ALWAYS STILL'),
 '“As waters enter the ocean, which is ever being filled yet stays unmoved, so the one whom all desires enter in the same way attains peace; not the one who craves desires.”',
 [('āpūryamāṇam','<b>being filled</b>'),('acala-pratiṣṭham','<b>unmoved in its depth</b>'),('samudram āpaḥ praviśanti','<b>waters enter the ocean</b>'),('saḥ śāntim āpnoti','<b>he attains peace</b>'),('na kāma-kāmī','<b>not the desirer of desires</b>')],
 'A great image. Rivers pour into the sea; it does not overflow, nor dry in summer.',
 'Commentators say desires still “enter” the sage, through ordinary life, but they do not disturb him; they are absorbed. The contrast is with the <i>kāma-kāmī</i>, one who desires desires, always restless.',
 'Let wants come and go like rivers into a deep sea. Depth, not emptiness, is the goal.',
 'Be <b>like the ocean</b>: full and still.'),
X(71,'WITHOUT “MINE”, WITHOUT “I”',
 S('A sage walking freely with empty hands','P','light','NIRMAMA · NIRAHAṄKĀRA'),
 '“One who gives up all desires and moves about free from longing, without ‘mine’ and without ‘I’: he attains peace.”',
 [('vihāya kāmān yaḥ sarvān','<b>who gives up all desires</b>'),('pumān carati niḥspṛhaḥ','<b>moves about without longing</b>'),('nirmamaḥ','<b>without “mine”</b>'),('nirahaṅkāraḥ','<b>without “I”</b>'),('saḥ śāntim adhigacchati','<b>he attains peace</b>')],
 'The cure for chapter 1: Dhṛtarāṣṭra began with “mine”; Kṛṣṇa ends with “without mine, without I”.',
 'Two great words: <i>nirmama</i> and <i>nirahaṅkāra</i>. They are the heart of the Gītā’s ethics and return in 12.13 and 18.53. Commentators say “I” and “mine” are the root of all bondage.',
 'Try saying “ours” instead of “mine”, and see what changes. That is a start.',
 'Without “mine” and “I”, <b>there is peace</b>.'),
X(72,'THE STATE OF BRAHMAN',
 S('A path ending in a great light','Y','om','BRĀHMĪ STHITI'),
 '“This is the state of Brahman, Pārtha. Reaching it, one is not deluded. Established in it even at the hour of death, one attains the peace of Brahman.”',
 [('eṣā brāhmī sthitiḥ','<b>this is the state of Brahman</b>'),('na enām prāpya vimuhyati','<b>reaching it, one is not deluded</b>'),('sthitvā asyām anta-kāle api','<b>abiding in it even at the end</b>'),('brahma-nirvāṇam ṛcchati','<b>attains the peace of Brahman</b>')],
 'The chapter ends with the goal: the state of Brahman, freedom from delusion, and the peace of Brahman at death.',
 '<i>Brahma-nirvāṇa</i> appears here first and returns in 5.24–26. Commentators note “even at the end”: the state reached late in life still frees. Chapter 2 began with Arjuna deluded; it ends with the promise of a state beyond delusion.',
 'It is never too late. Peace reached even at the last moment is real peace.',
 'The goal: <b>the peace of Brahman</b>.'),
]
